<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Symon Petliura</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Simon Petliura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Symon Petliura (1879–1926) was a Ukrainian journalist and socialist politician who became the leading figure of the Ukrainian People's Republic. As commander of its army and from 1919 head of the Directorate, he fought the Bolsheviks and the Whites and in 1920 allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Troops under his authority were blamed for massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1919. Exiled after the Soviet victory, he was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/symon-petliura.jpg" alt="Symon Petliura" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Symon_Petlura_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
